\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The increment at the opposite point}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Take the points #{0} and #{1/2}. The shorter arc is
#{1/2}. The second formula of \ref{fdip-00BD} gives
##{\mathbb E\lvert f_0-f_{1/2}\rvert^2
=\frac{2\sinh^2(m/4)}{m\sinh(m/2)}.}
The double-angle formula
#{\sinh(m/2)=2\sinh(m/4)\cosh(m/4)} reduces the right-hand
side to #{\tanh(m/4)/m}. The same quantity is recovered from
the closed form
#{G(\delta)=\cosh(m(1/2-\delta))/(2m\sinh(m/2))} at the
endpoints #{0} and #{1/2}:
##{2\bigl(G(0)-G(1/2)\bigr)
=\frac{\cosh(m/2)-1}{m\sinh(m/2)}
=\frac{2\sinh^2(m/4)}{m\sinh(m/2)},}
where #{\cosh(m/2)-1=2\sinh^2(m/4)}. The two evaluations
agree.}
